<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moselle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Moselle is a river of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Luxembourg and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 545 kilometres long. Rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>, it flows past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, forms part of the border between Luxembourg and Germany and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>. Its valley, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> as its chief city, was important in Roman and medieval times and is known for its vineyards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moselle.jpg" alt="Moselle" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Areks · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schweicher_Annaberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Moselle rises at 715 m on the Col de Bussang, on the western slopes of the Ballon d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>. Of its length, 313 km lie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, 39 km form the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Luxembourg, and 208 km are wholly within Germany. It flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at the Deutsches Eck in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>, where it is the second largest tributary of the Rhine by volume after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aare">Aare</a>.</p><p>Below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> it separates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a> to the north-west from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a> to the south-east, in wide meanders cut deeply into the Rhenish Massif, the most striking being the Cochemer Krampen. Its largest tributaries are the Meurthe, the Saar and the Sauer. The Meurthe was once the upper course of the Moselle, until the Moselle captured the former upper reaches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name comes from the Celtic Mosela by way of the Latin Mosella, a diminutive of Mosa, the Latin name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, so that the Mosella was the Little Meuse. Tacitus first records the river in his Annals and Histories. About 371 the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ausonius">Ausonius</a>, a teacher at the imperial court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, made it the subject of his poem Mosella, of 483 hexameters, describing a journey from Bingen to Trier and the rich landscapes along the valley under Roman rule. After the division of Lotharingia in the later 10th century, Upper Lotharingia along the river was often called Mosellania.</p><p>From 1815 the Moselle formed the border between the Grand Duchy of Luxembourg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. In September 1944 it was a barrier to the Allied advance, and the American 80th Infantry Division won a bridgehead at Dieulouard, allowing armour to move on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a>. At the tripoint near Schengen the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schengen-agreement">Schengen Agreement</a> was signed in 1985.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Moselle valley between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and Thionville is an industrial area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>. Further downstream the terraced hillsides carry vineyards, producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riesling">Riesling</a>, Elbling, Müller-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgau">Thurgau</a> and other wines, with many castle ruins above the villages. A treaty of 27 October 1956 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Luxembourg led to the canalisation of the river, officially opened from Metz to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a> on 26 May 1964; 394 km are now navigable through 28 locks, up to Neuves-Maisons south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a>. The Moselle Commission, founded in 1962, has its seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moselle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 959 Archbishop Bruno of Cologne, brother of Otto I and duke of Lotharingia, divided it into Lower Lorraine in the north and Upper Lorraine along the <b>Moselle</b> and upper Meuse, entrusting the south to Frederick, count of Bar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Schengen Agreement that abolished border checks across much of Europe is named after a small wine-growing village in Luxembourg, where it was signed on a boat on the <b>Moselle</b> in 1985.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After about 450 BCE the princely seats were abandoned and power passed to warrior elites farther north and west, in the Marne, the <b>Moselle</b> and the Rhineland, who were buried with two-wheeled chariots.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>There is no agreement on exactly where it began, but there is broad consensus that its centre lay on the north-western edge of the Hallstatt world, north of the Alps, between the valleys of the Marne and <b>Moselle</b> and the nearby Rhineland.</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>His descendants, and from 1136 the counts of Namur and later Limburg lines, expanded the county through the Ardennes and <b>Moselle</b> valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-luxembourg">County of Luxembourg</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts ruled villages, vineyards and castles across the Hunsrück and the lower Nahe and <b>Moselle</b>, balancing between the archbishops of Trier and Mainz and the Electoral Palatinate.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-sponheim">County of Sponheim</a></li></ul></section>
